  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss. It is 3 times steeper left-right (x), so overshoot starts there: with no noise a run diverges once learning rate × 3 > 2, i.e. above about 0.67.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise per direction is Gaussian with spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b: 2 at b=1, exactly 0 at b=64.",
    "Arrows are drawn at true length (scale 1, bowl units). Orange: the next mini-batch step, where the ball will move. Grey: the full-data step, −learning rate × true slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 8 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl (diverged). Off-bowl points are drawn at the edge and capped in height; the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from the fixed seed, run number and step number, so Reset replays the same sequence; each New run uses the next noise values. The host runs 5 steps a second.",
    "With noise a step lowers the loss only on average; small batches can raise it on single steps and keep wandering near the bottom."
  ]
}